# An order-line schema of the shape an application actually accumulates:
# a surrogate line key alongside the natural one, an order header folded into
# the line, a customer with a unique login e-mail, a catalogue price, and a
# warehouse in a region.
#
#   line_uuid            surrogate key minted by the application
#   order_id, line_no    the natural key
#   sku, qty             what was ordered
#   unit_price           catalogue price, carried on the line
#   customer_id          who ordered
#   customer_email       their login, which is also unique
#   warehouse, region    where it ships from
#
# Every dependency below is one a developer would state in a design review.
# The questions the run answers are the ones that get argued about instead of
# decided: is the natural key really a key; is the surrogate one redundant;
# which of these columns are denormalised; and does the entity split anyone
# would draw on a whiteboard actually preserve the information and the
# constraints.
#
# 10 attributes, so the candidate-key claim is settled by sweeping all 1024
# subsets -- every one of them either contains a reported key or is issued a
# two-row counterexample relation that is then checked.

attributes line_uuid order_id line_no sku qty unit_price customer_id customer_email warehouse region

fd f_line_surrogate : line_uuid -> order_id line_no
fd f_line_natural   : order_id line_no -> line_uuid sku qty
fd f_order          : order_id -> customer_id warehouse
fd f_customer_email : customer_id -> customer_email
fd f_email_customer : customer_email -> customer_id
fd f_sku_price      : sku -> unit_price
fd f_warehouse      : warehouse -> region

# The split any designer would draw: one table per entity.
decomposition by_entity : (line_uuid order_id line_no sku qty) (order_id customer_id warehouse) (customer_id customer_email) (sku unit_price) (warehouse region)

# The split that looks reasonable and is not: joining lines to customers
# through the catalogue, on `sku`, which is a key of neither side.
decomposition on_sku : (order_id line_no line_uuid sku qty unit_price) (sku customer_id customer_email warehouse region)

# A four-step chain: the line determines the order, the order the warehouse,
# the warehouse the region.
expect implies       : order_id line_no -> region
# And through the surrogate key, all the way to the customer's login.
expect implies       : line_uuid -> customer_email

# A catalogue entry says nothing about who bought it.
expect notimplies    : sku -> customer_id
# A warehouse does not determine the orders shipped from it.
expect notimplies    : warehouse -> order_id

# The surrogate key and the natural key are BOTH candidate keys, and there
# are no others: the surrogate is not redundant, and the natural one is not
# a convention.
expect keys          : (line_uuid) (order_id line_no)

# Five dependencies have non-superkey determinants, so the table is a
# denormalisation; `f_order` puts non-prime attributes on the wrong side of
# a partial dependency, so it is not even in 3NF.
expect bcnf          : no
expect 3nf           : no

expect lossless      : by_entity
expect preserving    : by_entity

expect lossy         : on_sku
expect notpreserving : on_sku
